3). This supports our hypothesis that separating object representations\ninto global and relational features enhances the model to solve tasks efficiently. By separating irrelevant features, STEDIE is able to find optimal actions efficiently by predicting interactions and tracking objects more accurately.\nUnderstanding Causal Relationship. Table 4 summarizes the reconstruction performance compared to other methods. We compare STEDIE against OP3 as well as two baseline models provided by Kipf et al. (2020) with action vectors incorporated in the dynamics module, using reconstruction MSE as the evaluation metric. For OP3 and STEDIE, the models were trained to predict up to t = 5 timesteps. For other baseline models, the same dataset was broken into 5 different single-step rollouts. As the table shows, our method achieves the lowest MSE for most cases, outperforming baseline models which were trained using reconstruction-based loss. This suggests that, compared to other methods, STEDIE understands not only the scenes but has learned the causal relationship between objects, leading to low reconstruction MSE even when rolling out longer into the future.\nFigure 4 shows the outcome of a single rollout of each object into all possible directions. The figure shows the ability of STEDIE to understand causal relationships. In this example, the model needs\nto comprehend the relationship for pushing (1) dark blue square (object 1) towards the left or (2) light blue circle (object 2) towards the right. The figure shows that STEDIE is the only model that has captured this causal relationship. However, STEDIE reconstructs the light blue object as square-shaped instead of a circle, suggesting room for further improvement."
